e  thing but i had  bigger portions of everything and i  think i ate like a half a loaf of bread  before the 15 hour fast before bed  even after eating more calories than  usual  i was still hungry at night which i  guess is a really great reminder to us  all  to eat our fiber fiber  is just so important for promoting  satiety while eating food that you  actually  like is so important for that emotional  satisfaction factor as well  neither of which i was getting on this  diet  but anyways i got her done and was on to  another day of tests  and this time i was gonna make damn sure  that nothing was gonna break so then  it was a waiting game  so i just got the call i got my results  back and not surprising they  are positive and you think i was like  upset about this like a positive  diagnosis for anything is usually  kind of a scary deal but in this case it  means that  you know there's something there and  there's something we can do about it  um and there's actual you know  medications that can help treat this  it's not just necessarily something i'm  gonna have to live with  for the rest of my life like something  just like straight up  mysterious like ibs so i'm actually  really relieved and my results showed  that  it was actually methane bacteria  dominant which was  which was surprising to me based on my  symptoms  because methane bacteria usually results  in  more constipation whereas hydrogen  bacteria  tends to be more diarrhea tmi  i know but that's typically what we see  clinically  but the hydrogen bacteria can actually  be masked when  methane bacteria is present because the  methane bacteria feeds on the hydrogen  bacteria  which can make the hydrogen numbers look  lower than they actually are so it's  actually quite possible that i have a  bit of both  but anyways the treatment in this case  is the same so i'm really looking  forward to  getting started on the protocol so my  official sibo treatment plan  was to be as follows i would start the  process with  two weeks on a biofilm disruptor now  biofilm is  an extracellular matrix that protects  the bacteria  from stressors like an antibiotic so  think of it like the beehive protecting  the colony inside  you ideally want to break down the  biofilm before you attack the colony so  to speak  and side note before you come at me i'm  not suggesting  that we kill bees i love bees we need  bees but we don't need  sibo anyways there ultimately aren't a  lot of breastfeeding safe  options right now so we opted to use an  acetylcysteine aka  nak which is a more gentle disruptor  than some pharmaceutical options on the  market  next after two weeks trying to break  down the biofilm  i was to begin a two-week protocol of an  antibiotic called rifaximin  plus an herbal microbial called allymax  pro  which is basically a high potency  allicin supplement  which has been shown to help with sibo  particularly methane dominant sibo  as an adjunct again there are stronger  options for this  since most people do need to be taking  two antibiotics to really kill off the  bacteria  but of course they aren't safe in  pregnancy  so we had to take like a gentler root  now this was actually the fun part  because it meant that i got to eat a lot  of the fodmaps that i've been  pulling back on since the theory goes  that  well-fed bacteria are more active  and therefore easier to kill now you'll  also find practitioners who recommend  the opposite  and that is to starve the bacteria but  generally  this is a recommendation for folks doing  an all-natural  herbal protocol which typically will  take  a bit longer to see results anyways  i happily ate my cauliflower  and watermelon and broccoli and brussels  sprouts  and my spicy barbecue baked beans and  gazpacho with so much raw garlic in it  that it would kill a vampire  and man it felt  so good emotionally  and also physically as well oh my god  guys  total tmi here but holy no pun intended  i did not know that so much poop could  come out  of a human and it feels amazing  i actually feel like i've got a better  appetite now as well because i have just  like  more room in my stomach for food  and i also don't feel so sluggish after  every single meal i'm not going to show  you like comparative body shots because  i don't want  this video to be just like about getting  a flat stomach but i will say that my  stomach is like  noticeably different as well i actually  just assumed that my stomach was just  like  my post baby belly slash uterus and i  was always like totally fine with that i  am  in awe at what my body has done and  accomplished  but i'm actually now starting to realize  that you know what  a lot of that was probably or is  probably  bloating so yeah this has been a  remarkable  difference here and i'm just hoping that  it keeps up and it really did after two  weeks of treatment i was  feeling super great and probably about  60 percent better  so we decided to do another two weeks of  treatment  to kill off the rest of bacteria but  that's when  it started to go downhill so  i'm on my third week so basically the  second round of antibiotics  and it's like they're not working but  i'm definitely plateauing  like i i was doing this in terms of  feeling better and now i'm doing  so i mean i don't know what's going on  i'm taking the same drug you'd think  that  things would continue to move in a  positive way but looks like we've  reached  a plateau and i was never able to  feel that good again after the fourth  week i moved on to the third  maintenance stage which was to come off  of the antibiotics and start a  prokinetic agent to promote motility and  prevent the bacteria from pooling  and flourishing basically a prokinetic  stimulates and coordinates  gastrointestinal motility to help  support  what we call the migratory motor complex  or mmc so the mmc moves bacteria  down the large intestine every 130  minutes or so  between meals and not having an adequate  or strong enough  mmc is